    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Chemistry-climate interactions",
    "problem": "Climate models in large intercomparison projects often use simplified representations of stratospheric ozone that are inconsistent with specific forcing scenarios, ignoring crucial chemical-circulation feedbacks and introducing significant biases of ~20% into global warming projections and estimates of climate sensitivity.",
    "method": "To improve global warming assessments, the authors propose that climate models must use model- and scenario-consistent representations of stratospheric ozone, either through fully interactive chemistry or specific prescribed data sets, to account for crucial ozone-circulation feedbacks that are otherwise ignored.",
    "task": "The task is to investigate the impact of interactive versus prescribed stratospheric ozone representations in a climate model on global warming projections, revealing a large ozone-circulation feedback that significantly alters estimates of effective climate sensitivity.",
